The online campaigns call for what supporters describe as "conscious purchasing," encouraging consumers to support hijab-observing vendors.&#160;
A female lawyer in Tehran told DW that such campaigns risk deepening social divisions rather than encouraging adherence to a social norm.&#160;
"This is a deliberate attempt to categorize people by identity and put them against one another," she said under condition of anonymity for safety concerns.
She argues that encouraging economic pressure against people solely because of their appearance goes beyond an individual's right to choose where to shop.&#160;
"When economic sanctions are imposed on them [the regime], they call it pressure, injustice and economic warfare," she said. "But when they use the same logic domestically to force part of society to change its behavior, it becomes 'support for values.'"&#160;
She also questioned the assumption that women who conform to one particular dress code uniquely represent Iranian social values.&#160;
"The reality of Iranian society is much more complex than that," she said.&#160;
Accounts posted by Iranian women suggest that dress-code enforcement can also appear in everyday services, with some women reporting on social media being denied access to clinics and banks for not wearing a headscarf.&#160;
Shirin Shams&#160;is an exiled Iranian political activist and director of the Women's Revolution Organization, which advocates for secularism, women's liberation, and human rights in Iran.
She told DW the recent campaigns targeting women who ditch the compulsory hijab are&#160;an attempt to revive social divisions that were weakened during the "Woman, Life, Freedom" movement.
"The Islamic Republic has tried for years to divide women according to their clothing," the Germany-based activist said.
She describes the compulsory hijab as one of the Iranian state's most important ideological lines and argues that the widespread presence of women without headscarves shows how far social reality has moved beyond official expectations.&#160;
The renewed campaigns, in her view, are about more than clothing, representing an effort to reconstruct a boundary between women who comply and those who do not.&#160;
The death 22-year-old Jina Mahsa Amini in 2022 became symbolic for the Iranian regime's oppression of women Image: UGC
Saeed Paivandi, a professor at the University of Lorraine in France, says the conflict reflects a broader struggle over social control.&#160;
He told DW that Iranian authorities have spent years creating distinctions between women considered "properly" veiled and those officially labeled "badly veiled" or "unveiled."&#160;
"The government is once again turning women's bodies into a social, moral and even security issue," Peyvandi said.&#160;
A woman without a headscarf is not interpreted merely as someone making an individual choice, he said. For parts of the political establishment, she can also represent a visible challenge to an ideological order.&#160;
'I will be living proof of the violent oppression in Iran'
