While Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian has repeatedly spoken about social cohesion and national unity, conservative judicial and political figures&#160;continue to demand tougher enforcement of dress regulations.&#160;
This produces an uneasy balance and exposes differences within Iran's political establishment.&#160;
Parts of the establishment appear aware that a return to large-scale confrontations between police and women could carry significant social and political costs. Others are unwilling to accept the normalization of women appearing in public without wearing a hijab.&#160;
The result is neither the old system of comprehensive street enforcement nor an end to compulsory dress rules.&#160;
Instead, pressure can appear intermittently through courts, workplaces, businesses, administrative procedures and social campaigns.&#160;
In 2022, protests erupted across Iran after a woman died in the custody of the so-called 'morality police' Image: AFP
Paivandi believes direct coercion has reached its limits and that supporters of Islamic dress codes are looking for alternative ways to enforce conformity.&#160;
That could shift part of the burden from state institutions to society itself.&#160;
A bank employee, clinic receptionist, employer, shopkeeper or customer can become involved in enforcing standards even without a police officer being present.&#160;
This makes the boundary between law, personal discretion and ideological pressure increasingly unclear.&#160;
Paivandi also warns that linking religion to humiliation, exclusion and coercion could further alienate younger Iranians from religious institutions.&#160;
Women who choose to wear a hijab may also be drawn into a confrontation they did not choose, he said, if their clothing is presented as a marker of political loyalty.&#160;

Expansion was a key issue at 'Berlin Process' talks in candidate country Montenegro this month seeking to further Western Balkan integration into the EU Image: Regierung von Montenegro Advertisement
The European Commission on Tuesday published a document called "Preparing for a wider Union," which sought to lay out new proposals on "the policy orientations and actions needed to prepare the [European] Union for future enlargements ."&#160;
Commission President Ursula von der Leyen said that "Europe's future depends on the choices we make today," as she tries to encourage progress at the EU's leaders' summit this month and in other upcoming talks on the matter.&#160;
The EU's executive proposed an array of changes — including a gradual integration process for would-be members, new safeguards "to ensure that commitments undertaken during accession negotiations continue to be fulfiled steadily after accession," and changes to voting rules.
They were trying to strike a balance between simplifying the accession process, incentivizing candidate countries to continue to pursue it, and allaying concerns among existing members about potential risks or costs of expansion.&#160;
Ukraine is the newest EU candidate country, seeking to accelerate the process amid Russia's invasion Image: Valentyn Ogirenko/REUTERS
In the quarter century after the end of the Cold War, the EU rapidly grew from a union of 12 member states to 28, before dipping back down to 27 when the UK left following 2016's Brexit referendum.&#160;
But the process has&#160;ground to a halt ever since, with a series of candidate countries stuck in lengthy negotiations and little apparent appetite among members to continue to expand.&#160;
In recent years, amid Russia's invasion of Ukraine and signs of the US vying for increased influence in parts of Europe, theoretical interest in expansion has picked up once more . But realizing the idea, with strict rules governing the accession process and the need for unanimous support among existing members for many core steps, has remained challenging.&#160;
EU enlargement: Montenegro on track to join bloc by 2028
