What did the Commission&#160;say about expansion?&#160;
Von der Leyen said the Commission was trying to prepare institutions, adapt policies and adjust budgets to reflect shared realities to better facilitate and accommodate expansion.
"Enlargement of our Union&#160;is a geopolitical imperative," she said. "Today, we are laying the groundwork to ensure that a larger Union is also a stronger one: more resilient, more effective, and firmly anchored in the values and rules that define us."&#160;
She said that as countries progress on their path towards reform,&#160;"the EU must be ready to welcome them."&#160;
Enlargement Commissioner Marta Kos said that Brussels' assessment "shows that the EU can accommodate a larger membership." But "there are areas where we see challenges we need to address," she acknowledged.
"One is the rule of law and sincere cooperation, where we are proposing stronger possibilities to prevent backsliding, including reducing funding or restricting voting rights," Kos said.&#160;
To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video
What changes are&#160;the European Commission proposing?&#160;
The Commission lays out a number of proposed changes or policies designed to smooth the accession process, including but not limited to:&#160;
Moving from unanimity to qualified majority voting among members in a number of the less significant votes about advancing or progressing membership bids
It makes no mention of changing the current requirement for a unanimous vote about finalizing&#160;membership applications, however
Gradual and accelerated integration of candidate countries to "bring tangible benefits to citizens and businesses," but which would remain "conditional and reversible"
"Stronger safeguards against serious backsliding on commitements undertaken during the acession negotiations" in future accession treaties, which could "in the most serious cases" extend to&#160;the suspension of voting rights
Introducing "targeted arrangements" to "significantly limit financial support and market access" for sensitive agricultural products from candidate country Ukraine
Ukraine's vast agricultural sector had prompted some concerns among EU members looking at how the bloc's budget could handle expansion, given that agriculture is a rare policy area where the bulk of spending and subsidies are orchestrated at the EU level, not by national governments
All these proposals will be subject to further discussion among European leaders and governments, with the next major meeting being next week's EU leaders' summit. Should they gain traction and progress, no doubt after some alterations and amendments along the way, they would ultimately have to face and pass various voting procedures to be implemented.
Moldova's President Maia Sandu is also a staunch advocate of her country joining the EU Image: Vladislav Culiomza/REUTERS
What's the current situation with EU candidate countries?&#160;
A total of eight countries currently have formal&#160;candidate status, meaning they are seeking to join the EU and have at least begun the often lengthy process that involves.&#160;
Some hope to join the bloc sooner rather than later, not least frontrunner Montenegro , after years of negotiations. Kos told DW last week that Montenegro was currently on track to become the next EU member by 2028.&#160;
Others, like Turkey, have nominally been candidates for decades but have made no discernible progress for years.&#160;
Ukraine is the newest addition to the list , having only gained full candidate status in 2024, but it is also the one with the most pressing motivation to hurry the process along amid Russia's invasion.
Albania, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Georgia, Moldova, North Macedonia and Serbia are also all seeking membership.&#160;
A ninth country, Kosovo, is recognized as a potential candidate but not a formal one —&#160;with not all EU members recognizing Kosovo's unilateral declaration of independence from Serbia .&#160;
